================================================================================
SCOPE — which files this covers
================================================================================

This MIT licence applies ONLY to the inference runtime and its language
bindings, listed exhaustively below. The repository as a whole remains
GPL-3.0-or-later (see LICENSE), because the grapheme-to-phoneme layer embeds
espeak-ng, which is GPL-3.0. Nothing below embeds, links, or derives from it.

  mcu/include/snt_nano.h
  mcu/include/snt_tts.h
  mcu/include/snt_port.h
  mcu/src/snt_nano.c
  mcu/src/snt_tts.c
  mcu/src/snt_kernels_ref.c
  mcu/ports/host/snt_port_host.c
  mcu/ports/wasm/snt_port_wasm.c
  mcu/models/*/nano_q8_meta.h        (generated offset headers)
  mobile/sanotts.h
  mobile/sanotts.c
  mobile/swift/SanoTTS.swift
  mobile/kotlin/SanoTTS.kt
  mobile/kotlin/sanotts_jni.c
  mobile/dart/sanotts.dart
  mobile/CMakeLists.txt
  mobile/build_ios.sh
  pypkg/**                           (the `sanotts` PyPI package)

Everything else in the repository — in particular the espeak-ng ports under
mcu/ports/*/espeak*, mcu/ports/esp32s3/firmware/components/espeak-ng/,
mcu/ports/wasm/build_g2p.sh, and the training and evaluation tooling under
tools/ — stays GPL-3.0-or-later. `npmpkg/` also stays GPL-3.0-or-later,
because its `dist/` still ships espeak-ng compiled to WebAssembly.

pypkg/ carries third-party data under its own terms, recorded in
pypkg/sanotts/g2p_data/NOTICE.md: misaki's pronunciation dictionaries and the
out-of-vocabulary model are Apache-2.0. Neither is GPL, and neither is
espeak-derived.

================================================================================
WHY THIS SPLIT IS SOUND
================================================================================

The repository was GPL-3.0 on the stated grounds that "the pipeline builds on
piper + espeak-ng (both GPLv3)". Half of that is wrong: piper and
piper-phonemize are MIT. The copyleft obligation comes from espeak-ng alone.

The files above were audited for it. They contain no reference to espeak,
piper, or GPL, and no third-party source: the only "verbatim" note in the
runtime is a cross-reference to another file in this same repository. The
espeak-ng code lives entirely in the G2P and port layers, which are not
listed above and stay GPL-3.0.

pypkg/ was added to this list on 2026-09-04, once every voice it ships had a
grapheme-to-phoneme path that does not use espeak: misaki's Apache-2.0
dictionaries plus an Apache-2.0 out-of-vocabulary model for English, and rule
front ends written from the orthography for Indonesian and Vietnamese. Each
replacement was measured against the path it replaced rather than assumed
equivalent; the evidence is under experiments/evidence/. espeak was used only
as a black-box oracle to diff against — no espeak dictionary or rule file was
read into this repository, and nothing here was trained on espeak's output.

One trap found while doing it, recorded so nobody walks into it later: misaki
declares Apache-2.0 repository-wide, but misaki/vi.py is a port of vPhon,
which is GPL-3.0 — the two trans() functions are 90.5% line-identical with
33 comments surviving verbatim. Nothing from it is used here.

Two algorithm reimplementations are worth naming, since neither is a copy:

  * mcu/src/snt_nano.c reproduces PyTorch's MT19937 seeding, its 24-bit
    uniform, and normal_fill_16's Box-Muller so that a seeded rendering is
    reproducible across implementations. PyTorch is BSD-3-Clause; this is an
    independent implementation of the documented algorithm, not its source.
  * The decoder mirrors the ConvNeXt-1D and iSTFT-head structure of Vocos and
    the residual-block shape of VITS. Both are MIT. Again structure, not code.

Upstream licences, checked rather than assumed:

  rhasspy/piper             MIT
  rhasspy/piper-phonemize   MIT
  espeak-ng/espeak-ng       GPL-3.0   <- the only copyleft input
  hexgrad/kokoro            Apache-2.0
  gemelo-ai/vocos           MIT
  jaywalnut310/vits         MIT

Model weights are distributed separately from this source, are distilled from
Apache-2.0 (Kokoro) and MIT (piper) teachers, and carry their own terms in
each voice package's meta.json and README.

This is a statement of the project's licensing intent, not legal advice.
